SECTION 1 — CORE BEND MATH FORMULAS
================================================================================

All formulas are for 90-degree air bending on a press brake.

  OSS / OSB (Outside Setback):
    OSS = tan(45°) × (R + T)  =  R + T     [for 90° bend, tan(45°)=1]
    Also written: OSB = R + T

  BA (Bend Allowance):
    BA = (π/2) × (R + K × T)
    BA = (Angle × π / 180) × (R + K × T)   [general form]

  BD (Bend Deduction):
    BD = (2 × OSS) - BA
    BD = 2 × (R + T) - BA

  BA/2:
    BA/2 = offset from hard corner to bend centerline
    BA/2 = (π/4) × (R + K × T)

  Flat zone lengths (J flange, measured to bend centerlines):
    f2_flat (lip zone)  = nominal_f2 - BD/2     [blank edge → bend2 CL]
    f1_flat (leg zone)  = nominal_f1 - 3×BD/2   [used for blank sizing only]
    CL-to-CL leg span   = nominal_f1 - BD        [bend2 CL → bend1 CL]

  Flat zone lengths (L flange, for blank sizing / SideDef.f1):
    f1_flat             = nominal_f1 - OSS + BA   (= F1 - OSS + BA, same as §8 L_total)
    Equivalently:       nominal_f1 - BD/2 + BA/2   (NOT nominal_f1 - BD/2 alone;
                        the old BD/2-only form was short by BA/2 per leg.)

  Bend 1 CL placement (all active flanges, L and J):
    Always BA/2 from the hard corner toward the blank (e.g. bottom: y = fy0 - BA/2).

NOTE ON REFERENCE POINTS:
  - Our code measures flat zones to BEND CENTERLINES
  - Some references (Gemini, shop floor) measure to MATERIAL EDGE
  - Difference = BA/2 (e.g., 2.084" CL vs 1.9375" material edge for 0.1875" Al)
  - Both are correct; they describe the same physical geometry

NOTE ON OSS vs BD/2:
  - OSS = R + T = 0.3125" (for 0.1875" Al, r=0.125")
  - BD/2 = 0.1657"
  - OSS ≠ BD/2 — they are different quantities
  - OSS is used for: setback from mold line; §6 face-corner reference geometry (optional / not separate DXF loops today)
  - BD/2 is used for: flat zone deductions from nominal flange depth


